\section{From Effect Summaries to Ordering Edges}

If an IR consists only of value definitions, it is unclear what keeps two discarded calls to a logging routine in their original order. An effect summary tells the compiler \emph{what a computation may do}: read or write an abstract location, allocate, raise, or perform I/O. An explicit state dependency tells it \emph{which version of state a computation observes} or which observable operation must complete first. One is not a substitute for the other. Two accesses may both have a write effect yet commute on disjoint objects; two I/O operations with no value dependence may need a common ordering edge. An unknown call needs a conservative summary and must be kept on every state chain it might affect.

Peyton Jones and Shields use monads in \textit{Bridging the Gulf} to expose semantic differences between strict and lazy source languages. At a lower level, Braun, Buchwald, and Zwinkau's \textit{FIRM---A Graph-Based Intermediate Representation} describes memory dependences carried by graph edges. These solve related but different problems: the first makes computation interfaces explicit; the second lets optimizers inspect or relax concrete dependences directly. The dossier's Markdown Chapters~16, \textit{Effect-Aware IR}, and 17, \textit{Memory as an Explicit IR}, separate the design choices along similar lines.

\section{Worked Example: Crossing an Unrelated Store}

Consider a sequential, nonvolatile fragment with valid, aligned word-sized accesses and no asynchronous observers. In this notation a store defines a fresh memory version; a load observes a version without changing it:

\begin{verbatim}
m1 = store(m0, p, 42)
m2 = store(m1, q, 17)
v  = load(m2, p)
return (v, m2)
\end{verbatim}

If the accessed bytes at \texttt{p} and \texttt{q} are disjoint, \texttt{v} may be replaced by \(42\), but \texttt{m2} must still be produced. The proof walks backwards: the second store leaves the bytes at \texttt{p} unchanged, while the first store establishes exactly the bytes read by the load. Without disjointness, \texttt{q} might equal \texttt{p}, giving \(17\), or partially overlap it. The assumptions about valid accesses matter independently: eliminating a load whose read can fault is unsound even when its bytes could be computed. In a concurrent language the corresponding proof needs the memory model as well; atomic or volatile operations may carry observations that this example does not model.

Diwan, McKinley, and Moss evaluate type-based disambiguation for Modula-3 in \textit{Type-Based Alias Analysis}. Type compatibility alone proved substantially less precise than analyses also using field or flow information. Reusing such a fact in an IR requires preserving the assumptions on which it was derived. For instance, a frontend that has erased the distinction between ordinary typed field access and unchecked byte access cannot simply assert non-aliasing from a low-level pointee type.

\section{Regions as Lifetime Boundaries}

A region can mean an alias partition, a storage lifetime, or a syntactically nested computation; these are different notions. A lifetime region may be modeled as a scope that creates a fresh name \(\rho\), gives its body an allocation capability, and retires that capability on exit. A pointer of type \(\mathrm{Ref}_{\rho}(T)\) cannot survive the exit by being returned, saved into longer-lived storage, or captured by an escaping closure. Checking only the explicit return type misses the second and third paths unless the type or effect system also tracks storage and captures. Bowman's study of ANF explains why scoped region allocation can make apparently administrative movements unsafe: commuting a continuation across a scope can change when its code executes relative to retirement of the region.

At a branch, memory versions from distinct paths must be combined by control selection, not by equating the states. A verifier can check that a memory use has an available definition, that a join has a matching input for each predecessor, and that each operation's state signature is consistent with its summary. Splitting one global chain into per-partition chains then needs an additional semantic argument: accesses from different chains must really be reorderable, and calls that can access several partitions must account for them all. The graph can document the ordering decision, but its well-formedness alone does not prove the alias facts that justify it.
